        "A computer is a machine that does three things in a loop: fetch an instruction, decode it, execute it. Everything else — operating systems, browsers, AI models — is built on top of that loop.",
        "Four components carry the weight: the CPU (executes instructions), RAM (fast, temporary working space), storage (slow, permanent), and the GPU (thousands of small cores doing the same maths in parallel)." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "The CPU is fast but has almost no memory of its own. It reads instructions and data from RAM. RAM is volatile: cut the power and it is gone. Storage (SSD/HDD) survives a reboot but is orders of magnitude slower than RAM.",
        "This is why 'opening' a file copies it from storage into RAM first. It is also why a machine with too little RAM starts swapping to disk and feels slow even with a fast CPU.",
        "The GPU matters for you specifically: a CPU has a handful of powerful cores optimised for varied instructions; a GPU has thousands of weak cores optimised for doing the same multiplication on huge blocks of numbers. Neural networks are almost entirely large matrix multiplications, which is why AI training and local model inference live on GPUs.",
        "A fourth category has become common enough to name: the NPU (Neural Processing Unit), a chip built specifically for AI math rather than adapted for it. It trades flexibility for efficiency — running AI inference using far less power than a CPU or GPU doing the same work. NPUs now ship in most new Windows laptops and in every Apple Silicon Mac, and Windows Task Manager shows one as a fifth meter alongside CPU, Memory, GPU and Disk." ] },
