      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "A program is a file on disk. A process is that program loaded into RAM and executing, with its own memory space and an ID. Running the same program twice creates two processes that cannot read each other's memory — this isolation is a security boundary.",
        "The OS also enforces permissions: which user may read, write or execute which file. On Linux and macOS this is the read/write/execute model you will meet again on any server you deploy to.",
        "The terminal is not a different computer; it is a text interface to the same OS services the graphical interface uses. Learning it is not optional once you touch servers, Git, Python environments or Ollama." ] },
