  {
    day: 3,
    title: "Files, Data & Storage",
    sub: "computer",
    objectives: [
      "Explain the difference between a file format and a file extension.",
      "Choose an appropriate format for text, tabular, structured and media data.",
      "Describe what encoding is and why it breaks things.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A file is a named sequence of bytes. Nothing more. The extension (.csv, .png, .mp4) is a hint to the operating system, not a guarantee about the content.",
        "What actually makes a file readable is the format: an agreed convention for what those bytes mean." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Text formats (txt, csv, json, md, xml) store characters. You can open them in any editor and read them. Binary formats (png, mp4, xlsx, pdf, gguf) store structured bytes that need a specific program to interpret.",
        "Encoding is the map from characters to bytes. UTF-8 is the modern default and handles every language. The classic bug — Indonesian or accented characters showing as garbage — is almost always an encoding mismatch: written as UTF-8, read as something else.",
        "For your work, three formats carry disproportionate weight: CSV for tabular data, JSON for structured data exchanged between systems, and Markdown for documentation and AI prompts. You will meet all three constantly from Phase 2 onwards." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
