  {
    day: 4,
    title: "Internet Fundamentals",
    sub: "network",
    objectives: [
      "Trace what happens between typing a URL and seeing a page.",
      "Explain IP, DNS and HTTP in terms of the job each one does.",
      "Read a URL and identify its parts.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "The internet is not one network. It is a set of independent networks that agreed on shared rules (protocols) for passing packets to each other.",
        "Three of those rules matter today: IP (addressing), DNS (naming), HTTP (requesting documents and data)." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Every device reachable on a network has an IP address. Humans do not remember numbers, so DNS acts as a directory that translates a name (docs.python.org) into an address. If DNS fails, the site is fine but unreachable by name — which is why 'the internet is down' is often actually a DNS problem.",
        "HTTP is the request/response protocol on top. Your browser sends a request with a method (GET, POST), a path, and headers. The server returns a status code (200 OK, 404 not found, 401 unauthorised, 500 server error) and usually a body.",
        "HTTPS is the same protocol wrapped in TLS encryption. It protects the content in transit; it does not make the destination trustworthy. A padlock means private, not safe." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Open your browser's developer tools, Network tab, and reload any page. Look at one request: method, status code, size, time.",
        "Find one request that returned something other than 200 and read its status code." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Take the URL https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/index.html and label each part: scheme, host, path. Then predict what changes when a query string ?q=json is added." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Why does a status code exist at all? What would break if servers only returned a body?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "Reading a failure correctly",
      body: "An LMS integration stops working. The team says 'the API is broken'. The network tab shows status 401 on every call. 401 means unauthorised — the request reached the server and the server understood it, but rejected the credentials. That is an authentication problem, likely an expired token, not a broken API. The status code turned a vague complaint into a specific hypothesis in seconds.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Status code triage",
      steps: [
        "Write what each of these means and who is responsible: 200, 301, 401, 403, 404, 429, 500.",
        "For 429 and 500, note what a client should do differently in each case.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Explain why an API key sent in a URL query string is a worse idea than sending it in a header, using what you now know about how requests travel and get logged.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Internet bukan satu jaringan, melainkan banyak jaringan yang sepakat memakai aturan bersama. Tiga aturan yang dipakai hari ini: IP untuk pengalamatan, DNS untuk menerjemahkan nama menjadi alamat, dan HTTP untuk meminta dokumen serta data. Browser mengirim request dengan method (GET, POST), path, dan header; server membalas dengan status code — 200 berhasil, 404 tidak ditemukan, 401 tidak berwenang, 500 server bermasalah — plus isi. HTTPS adalah HTTP yang dibungkus enkripsi TLS.",
      salahPaham: "Gembok HTTPS berarti sambungannya privat, bukan tujuannya tepercaya. Situs penipuan juga bisa punya gembok. Dan keluhan \"internetnya mati\" sering sebenarnya DNS yang gagal: situsnya sehat, cuma tidak bisa dijangkau lewat namanya.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "DNS", id: "sistem penamaan yang menerjemahkan nama domain ke alamat IP" },
        { en: "status code", id: "kode status: angka yang menyatakan hasil sebuah request HTTP" },
        { en: "HTTPS", id: "HTTP yang dienkripsi dengan TLS; melindungi isi selama pengiriman" },
        { en: "IP address", id: "alamat IP: identitas numerik sebuah perangkat di jaringan" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "What is DNS?", source: "Cloudflare Learning Center", topic: "Networking", difficulty: "Beginner", minutes: 20, url: "https://www.cloudflare.com/learning/dns/what-is-dns/", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Penjelasan teknis tapi mudah dibaca tentang cara DNS menerjemahkan nama domain menjadi alamat IP.",
          inti: ["DNS adalah sistem direktori internet.", "Resolver, root server, TLD server, authoritative server.", "Hasil DNS di-cache untuk mempercepat akses.", "Kegagalan DNS sering terlihat seperti situs mati padahal server sehat."],
          kenapa: "Banyak masalah integrasi dan hosting sebenarnya adalah masalah DNS, bukan masalah aplikasi.",
          hubungan: "Melengkapi konsep alamat IP hari ini dan dipakai lagi saat membahas server dan cloud." } },
      { title: "HTTP overview and status codes", source: "MDN Web Docs", topic: "Web protocols", difficulty: "Beginner–Intermediate", minutes: 35, url: "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi referensi resmi tentang protokol HTTP: metode, header, status code, dan cara kerja request-response.",
          inti: ["HTTP bersifat request-response dan stateless.", "Metode: GET, POST, PUT, DELETE.", "Status code memberi tahu hasil dan siapa yang bermasalah.", "Header membawa metadata seperti autentikasi dan tipe konten.", "HTTPS = HTTP + enkripsi TLS."],
          kenapa: "Setiap pemanggilan API — termasuk API model AI — adalah request HTTP. Memahami ini membuat debugging jauh lebih cepat.",
          hubungan: "Prasyarat langsung untuk Day 5 (client-server) dan Day 7 (API)." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "DNS", q: "What job does DNS do?", opts: ["Encrypts traffic between browser and server", "Translates human-readable domain names into IP addresses", "Stores website content closer to users", "Assigns IP addresses to devices on a local network"], a: 1, e: "DNS is the naming layer. Encryption is TLS; local address assignment is DHCP; caching content near users is a CDN." },
      { t: "mc", c: "HTTP status codes", q: "An API call returns 401. What does this tell you?", opts: ["The server crashed", "The endpoint does not exist", "The request was understood but the credentials were missing or rejected", "The client sent too many requests"], a: 2, e: "401 is authentication. 404 is a missing resource, 429 is rate limiting, 500 is a server-side failure." },
      { t: "tf", c: "HTTPS", q: "A padlock icon in the browser means the website is trustworthy.", a: false, e: "HTTPS guarantees the connection is encrypted and the certificate matches the domain. It says nothing about the intent of the site owner." },
      { t: "sc", c: "HTTP status codes", q: "Your integration starts returning 429 during bulk imports. What is the right response?", opts: ["Retry immediately in a tight loop", "Slow down and retry with backoff, respecting the rate limit", "Switch to HTTP instead of HTTPS", "Send the requests without an API key"], a: 1, e: "429 means you exceeded a rate limit. Retrying harder makes it worse; backing off and pacing requests is the correct behaviour." },
      { t: "mc", c: "URL structure", q: "In https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/, which part is the host?", opts: ["https", "docs.python.org", "/3/tutorial/", "python"], a: 1, e: "Scheme is https, host is docs.python.org, path is /3/tutorial/." },
      { t: "sa", c: "HTTP status codes", q: "Why are status codes useful when debugging an integration?", a: { groups: [["cause", "penyebab", "sebab"], ["where", "di mana", "sisi mana", "letak", "lokasi"], ["client", "pengguna", "klien"], ["server", "peladen"], ["auth", "otentik", "izin"], ["categor", "kategori", "golong", "kelompok"]], need: 2, model: "They categorise the failure — client-side, authentication, missing resource, rate limiting or server error — so you know where to look instead of guessing." }, e: "The key idea: a status code localises the problem." },
    ],
  },
];

