    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A client asks. A server answers. Your browser, your phone app and a Python script are all clients; the machine holding the data and the business logic is the server.",
        "The important consequence: anything running on the client is visible to and modifiable by the user. Anything secret must live on the server." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Frontend is what runs in the browser: HTML for structure, CSS for presentation, JavaScript for behaviour. Backend is what runs on the server: application logic, database access, authentication, integrations.",
        "The API is the contract between them. The frontend does not know how the database is designed; it only knows which endpoints exist, what they accept and what they return. That separation is what allows a mobile app and a web app to share the same backend.",
        "This model is why you never put an AI provider API key in frontend code. Anyone can open developer tools and read it. The correct pattern is: browser to your backend, your backend to the model provider, key stored server-side." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Open any web application you use. In developer tools, watch the Network tab while you perform one action (search, save, log in).",
        "Identify: which request carried your action, what it sent, and what came back. That is the API boundary made visible." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "For a simple quiz feature, decide which side each item belongs on: rendering questions, storing answers, calculating the score, deciding whether the learner passed, showing the certificate." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
