    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "An API is a documented contract for talking to a system. You send a structured request to an endpoint; you get a structured response back, usually JSON.",
        "Every AI capability you will build on — Claude, Gemini, an embedding model, a vector database, your LMS — is reached this way." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Five things define any REST-style API call: the endpoint URL, the method (GET to read, POST to create or submit), the headers (content type, authentication), the request body, and the response (status code plus body).",
        "Authentication is usually an API key or a bearer token in a header. Rate limits cap how many calls you may make in a window; exceeding them returns 429. Costs are usually metered per call or per token.",
        "Reading documentation is a skill in itself. Work top-down: what does this endpoint do, what does it require, what does it return, what errors can it return, what does it cost. If you cannot answer those five, you are not ready to write code against it." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
