  {
    day: 8,
    title: "Databases",
    sub: "database",
    objectives: [
      "Explain tables, rows, columns, keys and relationships.",
      "Distinguish relational from document and vector databases by the problem each solves.",
      "Write a simple query in plain language and translate it to SQL structure.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A database is storage plus a query engine plus guarantees. The guarantees — consistency, durability, concurrent access — are the reason not to use spreadsheets or plain files for real systems.",
        "A relational database stores data in tables with defined columns, and links tables with keys." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "In a relational model, each row has a primary key that uniquely identifies it. A foreign key in another table points at that key, creating a relationship. Learners, courses and enrolments is the classic three-table shape: two entities and the relationship between them.",
        "SQL queries describe what you want, not how to get it: select these columns, from this table, where this condition holds, joined to that table. The engine decides the execution plan and uses indexes to avoid scanning everything.",
        "Not all data is relational. Document databases store JSON-like documents with flexible shapes. Vector databases store embeddings and answer 'what is semantically nearest to this' — that is the storage layer of RAG, which you reach at Day 19. Choosing a database is choosing which question you need answered fast." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "On paper, design three tables for a small LMS: learners, courses, enrolments. Mark primary and foreign keys.",
        "Write in plain language five questions the system must answer, for example 'which learners have not completed course X'." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Translate two of those questions into SQL structure: SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE ... JOIN ... . Correct syntax matters less than correct shape at this stage." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Why does storing enrolments as a comma-separated list inside the learner row eventually cause pain?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "The report nobody could run",
      body: "A training team stores completions as a text field: \"course A, course B\". Asking 'how many people completed course B in Q3' requires parsing strings, and any typo becomes an invisible data loss. Normalising into an enrolments table with one row per learner-course-date makes the question a two-line query. Data structure decisions determine which questions are cheap to ask later.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Model a small domain",
      steps: [
        "Design tables for learners, courses, enrolments, and quiz attempts.",
        "Decide where a post-test score belongs and why.",
