  {
    day: 12,
    title: "What AI Actually Is",
    sub: "ai-fundamentals",
    objectives: [
      "Place AI, machine learning and deep learning in the correct relationship.",
      "Explain the shift from rule-writing to pattern-learning.",
      "Decide whether a given problem is a reasonable candidate for AI at all.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "Artificial intelligence is the broad field of systems performing tasks that normally require aspects of human intelligence: perception, prediction, classification, generation, planning, decision support.",
        "Machine learning is one approach within AI, and deep learning is one family of techniques within machine learning. Nested circles, not synonyms." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Traditional software encodes rules a human wrote: if this condition, do that. Machine learning inverts it: you supply examples of inputs and desired outputs, and the training process finds parameters that map one to the other.",
        "That inversion is powerful where rules are hard to articulate — recognising a face, judging tone, summarising a document — and unnecessary where rules are clear. Computing a payroll deduction with a model would be absurd; the rule is known and must be exact.",
        "The trade-off is that a learned system has no guarantee of correctness. It has a measured performance on data resembling what it was trained on, and unknown behaviour outside it. This is the honest frame for every AI capability claim you will assess." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Take three tasks from your own work — tagging course content, calculating completion rates, drafting quiz questions — and classify each as rules-based, learnable, or hybrid. Justify each in one sentence." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "For one of the learnable tasks, write down: what data would be needed, what a wrong answer would cost, and how you would detect a wrong answer." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Complete this sentence honestly: 'AI is the wrong tool for this task when ...'" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "The wrong tool, confidently applied",
      body: "A team asks an LLM to calculate final course grades from raw scores. It usually works, which is the danger — occasionally it does not, and there is no error, only a wrong number stated fluently. Grade calculation is a known deterministic rule; it belongs in code. The model's appropriate job in that system is explaining the grade to the learner, not computing it.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "AI suitability screen",
      steps: [
        "List five tasks in your organisation someone has proposed using AI for.",
        "For each, answer: is the rule known? is data available? what does a wrong answer cost?",
        "Rank them by how appropriate AI actually is, and be ready to defend the bottom one.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Find one public claim about AI capability this week and rewrite it as a testable statement: what exactly would have to be measured, on what data, to verify it?",
    recap: {
      inti: "AI adalah lingkaran terluar, machine learning satu pendekatan di dalamnya, dan deep learning satu keluarga teknik di dalam machine learning — lingkaran bersarang, bukan tiga kata untuk hal yang sama. Perangkat lunak biasa menjalankan aturan yang ditulis manusia; machine learning membalik arahnya: lo menyediakan contoh input dan output yang diinginkan, lalu proses training mencari parameter yang memetakan keduanya. Pembalikan itu berguna ketika aturannya sulit dirumuskan, dan tidak perlu ketika aturannya sudah jelas.",
      salahPaham: "Sistem yang belajar tidak punya jaminan kebenaran. Yang ada hanya performa terukur pada data yang mirip data latihnya, dan perilaku yang tidak diketahui di luar itu. Menghitung potongan gaji dengan model akan konyol — aturannya sudah pasti dan hasilnya harus eksak.",
      istilah: [
