  {
    day: 16,
    title: "LLM Fundamentals: Tokens and Context Window",
    sub: "llm",
    objectives: [
      "Explain tokenisation and its effect on cost and limits.",
      "Describe what the context window holds and why it is finite.",
      "Diagnose problems caused by context loss or truncation.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A language model does not see characters or words. Text is split into tokens — frequent word pieces — and each token becomes a number the model processes.",
        "The context window is the total number of tokens the model can consider at once: instructions, conversation, retrieved documents, tool results and the response being generated." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Tokenisation is why English costs fewer tokens than Indonesian for the same meaning, why unusual names get split oddly, and why character counting tasks are surprisingly unreliable for models. It also determines your bill: API pricing is per token, input and output priced separately.",
        "The window is finite. When a conversation exceeds it, something must go — earlier turns are dropped or summarised. A model 'forgetting' an instruction from twenty messages ago is usually truncation, not disobedience.",
        "Position matters too: information buried in the middle of a very long context is used less reliably than material at the start or end — part of a broader pattern Anthropic calls context rot, where accuracy and recall quietly degrade as token count grows, position aside. This is a practical argument for retrieving a small, relevant set of documents rather than pasting everything you have." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Paste an English paragraph and its Indonesian translation into a tokeniser and compare token counts. Then tokenise a table and a code snippet and compare density." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Estimate the token cost of one run of a realistic task: system instructions plus a 3,000-word document plus a 500-word answer. Then calculate the monthly cost at 200 runs." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Why is 'just put the whole handbook in the prompt' a bad default, on both cost and quality grounds?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "The assistant that forgot the rules",
      body: "A support assistant is given detailed formatting rules in the first message. After a long conversation it stops following them. The cause is not drift of intent but truncation: the earliest messages fell out of the window. The fix is architectural — put durable rules in the system instructions, which are re-sent every turn, rather than in a chat message that ages out.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Token budget for a real feature",
      steps: [
        "Define a feature: AI feedback on learner short answers.",
        "Estimate input and output tokens per call.",
        "Compute cost per 1,000 learners and identify the biggest lever for reducing it.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Given a 300-page handbook and a 200k-token window, argue why retrieval is still preferable to pasting the whole document, using at least three distinct reasons.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Model tidak membaca kata, ia membaca token — potongan teks yang sering kali lebih pendek dari satu kata. Context window adalah batas total token yang bisa diproses dalam satu interaksi, dan isinya bukan cuma pertanyaan lo: instruksi sistem, riwayat percakapan, dokumen yang diambil, hasil tool, sampai jawaban yang sedang dihasilkan semuanya ikut mengisi. Kalau penuh, bagian paling awal terdorong keluar atau diringkas.",
      salahPaham: "\"Modelnya lupa\" adalah framing yang menyesatkan. Tidak ada yang dilupakan — informasinya memang sudah tidak dikirim lagi. Ini bukan keterbatasan ingatan, melainkan akibat aritmetika dari batas yang bisa lo hitung sendiri. Posisi juga berpengaruh: materi di tengah konteks yang sangat panjang dipakai lebih tidak andal daripada yang di awal atau akhir.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "token", id: "unit teks setelah dipotong; teks Indonesia umumnya memakan lebih banyak token daripada teks Inggris dengan makna yang sama — dan tagihan API dihitung per token" },
        { en: "context window", id: "jendela konteks: batas total token per satu interaksi" },
        { en: "truncation", id: "pemotongan bagian konteks tertua ketika jendela penuh" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "LLM Course", source: "Hugging Face", topic: "LLM Fundamentals", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 60, url: "https://huggingface.co/learn", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Kursus gratis tentang cara kerja model bahasa: tokenisasi, arsitektur transformer, dan penggunaan model.",
          inti: ["Tokenizer memecah teks menjadi unit sub-kata.", "Model memproses urutan token, bukan kata.", "Panjang konteks membatasi informasi yang bisa dipertimbangkan.", "Model dasar vs model instruksi.", "Praktik langsung dengan pustaka terbuka."],
          kenapa: "Token adalah satuan biaya, batas, dan performa pada setiap aplikasi LLM.",
          hubungan: "Menerjemahkan arsitektur transformer Day 14 menjadi konsep praktis sehari-hari." } },
      { title: "Gemini API documentation", source: "Google", topic: "LLM Fundamentals", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 30, url: "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi resmi API model bahasa Google, termasuk penjelasan token, batas konteks, dan parameter permintaan.",
          inti: ["Struktur permintaan dan respons API model.", "Perhitungan token input dan output.", "Batas konteks per model.", "Parameter yang memengaruhi keluaran."],
          kenapa: "Dokumentasi resmi adalah sumber paling akurat untuk batas dan harga yang sering berubah.",
          hubungan: "Menggabungkan keterampilan API Day 7 dengan konsep LLM hari ini." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "Tokens", q: "What is a token?", opts: ["A single character", "A word", "A frequent chunk of text, often a word piece, that the model processes as one unit", "An API authentication key"], a: 2, e: "Tokens are sub-word units. Note the vocabulary collision with authentication tokens, which are unrelated." },
      { t: "tf", c: "Context window", q: "The context window includes only the user's latest message.", a: false, e: "It covers system instructions, conversation history, retrieved content, tool results and the generated output together." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Context window", q: "An assistant stops following formatting rules given at the start of a long conversation. Best explanation?", opts: ["The model is disobeying", "Early messages were truncated out of the context window", "The API key expired", "The temperature was set too low"], a: 1, e: "Durable instructions belong in the system prompt, which is re-sent each turn, rather than in an ageing chat message." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Cost", q: "Why does the same meaning cost more tokens in Indonesian than in English for many models?", opts: ["Indonesian text is always longer", "Tokenisers are trained on corpora where English word pieces are more efficiently represented", "Indonesian uses more punctuation", "The model translates internally first"], a: 1, e: "Token efficiency reflects the tokeniser's vocabulary, which is shaped by training data distribution." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Context window", q: "Why prefer retrieving relevant passages over pasting an entire handbook?", opts: ["Cost, latency, and more reliable use of relevant material", "It is the only technical option", "Long documents cannot be uploaded", "Retrieval removes hallucination completely"], a: 0, e: "It is cheaper, faster and usually more accurate — though retrieval reduces rather than eliminates hallucination." },
      { t: "sa", c: "Tokens", q: "Name two things token count directly affects.", a: { groups: [["cost", "price", "bill", "biaya", "harga", "tagihan"], ["limit", "context", "batas", "konteks"], ["latency", "speed", "kecepatan", "cepat", "lambat", "tanggapan"]], need: 2, model: "Cost of the API call and how much fits inside the context window; also latency." }, e: "Cost, limits and speed." },
    ],
  },
];

const CUR_E = [
  {
    day: 17,
    title: "Prompting, System Instructions and Structured Output",
    sub: "prompting",
    objectives: [
      "Write prompts with explicit goal, context, constraints and output format.",
      "Explain the role of system instructions versus user messages.",
      "Request structured output and validate it before use.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A prompt is not a magic phrase. It is the specification of a task: what to do, with what information, under what constraints, in what shape.",
        "Most prompt failures are specification failures — the request was ambiguous and the model resolved the ambiguity differently than you assumed." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Five elements do most of the work: goal, context, constraints, input, output format. Adding evaluation criteria — how you will judge the result — often improves output more than any phrasing trick.",
        "System instructions define durable behaviour: role, rules, tone, refusals. They are sent every turn, so they survive long conversations. User messages carry the specific task. Putting standing rules in a user message is the mistake that produced Day 16's forgotten formatting rules.",
        "Structured output means asking for JSON matching a defined schema so software can consume it. It is not a guarantee: you must parse defensively, validate against the schema, and decide what happens on failure. An application that assumes valid JSON will eventually break in production.",
        "Two techniques are worth naming because you will meet them in every vendor's documentation. Zero-shot means describing the task and nothing else; few-shot means including two or three worked examples of input and desired output. Examples beat adjectives: showing one well-formed quiz item usually does more than a paragraph describing what a good item looks like. The cost is context — every example is tokens you pay for on every call.",
        "The second is delimiters. When a prompt mixes instructions with content, mark the boundary explicitly — XML-style tags such as <document> and </document> are the convention in Anthropic's documentation, and any consistent marker works. Without a boundary, a learner's pasted text containing the words 'ignore the above' is indistinguishable from your instruction, which is the mechanism behind prompt injection you will meet again in AI security." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Write a prompt that turns a paragraph into three quiz items as JSON with fields question, options, answer_index, explanation.",
        "Run it three times and compare the results for consistency of shape, not just content." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Add explicit constraints — exactly four options, one unambiguously correct, no 'all of the above', explanation under 30 words — and rerun. Note which constraints changed the output most." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "What would your application do if the model returned prose instead of JSON? Write the fallback in one sentence." ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "Constraints beat phrasing",
      body: "A team iterates for days on wording to improve generated MCQs. The change that actually works is a written rubric in the prompt: four options, exactly one correct, distractors plausible to a partially-informed learner, no length cues, explanation citing the source sentence. Making the quality criteria explicit is what improves the output — the same thing that improves briefs given to human writers.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Prompt with a rubric",
      steps: [
        "Write quality criteria for a good learning explanation.",
        "Build a prompt containing goal, context, constraints, input and output format.",
        "Generate five outputs and score them against your criteria.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Design a prompt whose output your code can trust. Specify the schema, the validation step, and the behaviour when validation fails — including what the learner sees.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Prompt bukan mantra, melainkan spesifikasi tugas: tujuan, konteks, batasan, masukan, dan bentuk keluaran. Sebagian besar kegagalan prompt adalah kegagalan spesifikasi — permintaannya ambigu, lalu model menyelesaikan ambiguitas itu berbeda dari yang lo asumsikan. System instruction memuat perilaku yang tahan lama (peran, aturan, nada) dan dikirim ulang setiap giliran; user message memuat tugas spesifik. Aturan tetap yang ditaruh di user message akan hilang begitu percakapan memanjang.",
      salahPaham: "Meminta JSON bukan jaminan menerima JSON yang valid. Structured output harus tetap diurai secara defensif, divalidasi terhadap skema, dan punya rencana untuk kasus gagal. Catatan kedua: contoh mengalahkan kata sifat — dua-tiga contoh few-shot biasanya lebih berpengaruh daripada satu paragraf yang menjelaskan seperti apa hasil yang bagus, dengan ongkos token di setiap panggilan.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "system instruction", id: "instruksi sistem: aturan tetap yang berlaku sepanjang percakapan" },
        { en: "few-shot", id: "menyertakan beberapa contoh masukan dan keluaran yang diinginkan" },
        { en: "zero-shot", id: "hanya mendeskripsikan tugas tanpa memberi contoh" },
        { en: "structured output", id: "keluaran terstruktur seperti JSON agar bisa diproses program" },
        { en: "delimiter", id: "penanda batas antara instruksi dan konten, misalnya tag bergaya XML" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "Prompting best practices", source: "Anthropic", topic: "Prompt Engineering", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 40, url: "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi resmi tentang teknik menyusun prompt: instruksi jelas, contoh, penalaran bertahap, dan format keluaran.",
          inti: ["Jelas dan spesifik lebih baik daripada panjang.", "Contoh positif dan negatif membantu model.", "Meminta penalaran bertahap untuk tugas kompleks.", "Menentukan format keluaran secara eksplisit.", "Instruksi sistem untuk perilaku yang menetap."],
          kenapa: "Sumber resmi lebih dapat dipercaya daripada daftar 'trik prompt' yang beredar di media sosial.",
          hubungan: "Diverifikasi ulang pada sesi M15 Batch 4: alamat lama (docs.claude.com/.../overview) sudah dicoba langsung dan terbukti memang mengalihkan (302) ke platform.claude.com, seperti dugaan sesi back-verification sebelumnya. Tapi halaman overview yang dituju kini cuma halaman penunjuk, bukan lagi berisi tekniknya — isinya sendiri sudah dipindah ke halaman 'Prompting best practices' ini, yang ditemukan lewat tautan langsung di halaman overview tersebut (bukan tebakan) dan sudah dibuka: strukturnya cocok dengan ringkasan di atas (kejelasan, contoh, penalaran, format, dan sistem prompt masuk ke bagian 'Techniques for all current models'). Alamat yang disimpan diperbarui ke halaman ini karena di sinilah isinya benar-benar berada." } },
      { title: "Structured output and function calling", source: "Google", topic: "Prompt Engineering", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 30, url: "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi API tentang cara meminta keluaran terstruktur (JSON) dan mendefinisikan skema.",
          inti: ["Skema mendefinisikan bentuk keluaran yang diharapkan.", "Keluaran terstruktur memudahkan integrasi dengan kode.", "Validasi tetap diperlukan di sisi aplikasi.", "Menangani kegagalan parsing secara eksplisit."],
          kenapa: "Aplikasi nyata membutuhkan data yang bisa diproses program, bukan paragraf bebas.",
          hubungan: "Menggabungkan JSON (Day 3, Day 10) dengan prompting hari ini." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "Prompt structure", q: "Which set of elements most reliably improves prompt results?", opts: ["Politeness, urgency, repetition", "Goal, context, constraints, input, output format", "Longer sentences and more adjectives", "Asking the model to be confident"], a: 1, e: "Specification beats phrasing. Ambiguity is the main cause of unusable output." },
      { t: "mc", c: "System instructions", q: "Why put standing rules in system instructions rather than a chat message?", opts: ["They cost fewer tokens", "They are re-sent every turn and survive long conversations", "They cannot be overridden by anything", "The model reads them faster"], a: 1, e: "This is the architectural fix for rules being truncated out of a long conversation." },
      { t: "tf", c: "Structured output", q: "If you ask for JSON, you can safely assume valid JSON will always be returned.", a: false, e: "Treat it as likely, not guaranteed. Parse defensively, validate, and define failure behaviour." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Structured output", q: "Your application crashes when the model wraps JSON in explanatory text. Best fix?", opts: ["Ask the model more politely", "Validate and repair the response, with a defined fallback path on failure", "Increase the temperature", "Switch to a larger model and hope"], a: 1, e: "Robustness lives in your code. Model behaviour is probabilistic; your handling should not be." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Prompt structure", q: "Which addition typically improves generated assessment items most?", opts: ["A longer role description", "Explicit quality criteria for what a good item looks like", "Requesting more items", "Raising the temperature"], a: 1, e: "A rubric is a specification. It works for the same reason it works with human writers." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Prompt structure", q: "Your prompt describes a good quiz item in three sentences of adjectives, and the output is still inconsistent in shape. What is the most reliable next change?", opts: ["Add more adjectives describing quality", "Include two worked examples of a well-formed item and its explanation", "Raise the temperature so the model varies more", "Ask the model to try harder"], a: 1, e: "Few-shot examples specify the target concretely; adjectives leave the model to interpret them. The trade-off is the extra context those examples consume on every call." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Prompt structure", q: "Why should learner-submitted text be wrapped in explicit delimiters such as <document>...</document> inside a prompt?", opts: ["It compresses the text into fewer tokens", "It lets the model process the text faster", "It marks where instructions end and untrusted content begins, so text that looks like an instruction is not treated as one", "It is required by the JSON schema"], a: 2, e: "Without a boundary the model cannot distinguish your instructions from content that imitates instructions. This is the basic defence against prompt injection." },
      { t: "sa", c: "Prompt structure", q: "Why is 'make it better' a weak instruction?", a: { groups: [["ambigu", "vague", "tidak jelas", "kabur", "samar"], ["criteri", "kriteria", "ukuran"], ["specific", "spesifik", "rinci"], ["define", "definisi", "mendefinisikan", "menentukan"], ["measur", "terukur", "mengukur"], ["model menebak", "menebak sendiri", "menafsir", "interpretas", "resolve"]], need: 2, model: "It does not define the criteria for better, so the model resolves the ambiguity on its own terms." }, e: "Unspecified criteria produce unpredictable results." },
    ],
  },
  {
    day: 18,
    title: "Embeddings and Vector Search",
    sub: "embeddings",
    objectives: [
      "Explain what an embedding represents and why similarity is measured as distance.",
      "Describe chunking and why it determines retrieval quality.",
      "Distinguish semantic search from keyword search by failure mode.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "An embedding is a list of numbers representing a piece of text so that similar meanings sit close together in that numeric space.",
        "Once meaning is numeric, 'find related content' becomes 'find nearby vectors' — a computation, not a guess." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "An embedding model converts text into a fixed-length vector. Similarity is usually cosine distance. Keyword search matches characters, so a query about 'employee onboarding' misses a document titled 'new starter induction'; semantic search finds it because the meanings are close.",
        "The trade-off runs the other way too: semantic search can retrieve something topically near but factually wrong for the question, and it handles exact identifiers such as product codes poorly. Serious systems combine both.",
        "Chunking is the quiet determinant of quality. Chunks too large dilute the signal and waste context; too small lose the meaning that made them findable. Splitting on document structure — sections, headings — usually beats splitting on a fixed character count. Every chunk should carry metadata: source, section, date." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Take one policy document and chunk it two ways: fixed 500-character blocks, and by section heading. Compare which chunks would answer a specific question well." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Write five realistic questions a learner would ask about that document, and mark for each which chunking strategy retrieves the right passage." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Why can retrieval return a passage that is topically relevant but useless for answering the actual question?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "The chunk that lost its heading",
      body: "A knowledge assistant answers a question about parental leave with the rules for a different country, because the chunk containing the entitlement table was split away from the heading that named the jurisdiction. Nothing was wrong with the model. The retrieval unit had lost the context that gave it meaning. Chunk boundaries are a content design decision, which is why instructional designers are unusually well placed to get this right.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Design a chunking strategy",
      steps: [
        "Choose a real document set from your organisation.",
        "Define chunk boundaries, overlap and metadata fields.",
        "Justify each decision against the questions users will actually ask.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Explain why an embedding-based search over your course catalogue might return a beginner course for an advanced query, and propose two ways to fix it without changing the model.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Embedding adalah deretan angka yang mewakili sepotong teks sedemikian rupa sehingga makna yang mirip berada berdekatan dalam ruang angka itu; kemiripannya biasanya diukur sebagai cosine distance. Pencarian keyword mencocokkan karakter, jadi pertanyaan tentang \"employee onboarding\" akan meleset dari dokumen berjudul \"new starter induction\"; pencarian semantik menemukannya karena maknanya berdekatan. Chunking adalah penentu kualitas yang sering diabaikan: potongan terlalu besar mengencerkan sinyal, terlalu kecil menghilangkan makna yang membuatnya bisa ditemukan.",
      salahPaham: "Pencarian semantik bukan versi \"lebih pintar\" dari pencarian keyword — ia menukar satu jenis kegagalan dengan jenis lain. Ia bisa mengambil sesuatu yang setopik tapi salah fakta, dan buruk menangani identifier eksak seperti kode produk. Sistem serius menggabungkan keduanya.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "embedding", id: "representasi teks sebagai vektor angka yang menangkap makna" },
        { en: "cosine", id: "ukuran kedekatan arah dua vektor; dasar penilaian kemiripan makna" },
        { en: "chunking", id: "pemotongan dokumen menjadi bagian yang disimpan dan diambil" },
        { en: "metadata", id: "keterangan yang menyertai tiap potongan: sumber, bagian, tanggal" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "What is a vector database?", source: "Pinecone", topic: "Embeddings", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 30, url: "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/vector-database/", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Penjelasan tentang penyimpanan dan pencarian vektor: bagaimana embedding diindeks dan dicari berdasarkan kedekatan makna.",
          inti: ["Embedding disimpan sebagai vektor berdimensi tinggi.", "Pencarian dilakukan dengan ukuran kedekatan seperti cosine similarity.", "Indeks perkiraan (ANN) mempercepat pencarian skala besar.", "Metadata dipakai untuk memfilter hasil."],
          kenapa: "Basis data vektor adalah lapisan penyimpanan pada sistem RAG yang dipelajari besok.",
          hubungan: "Menyambungkan konsep basis data Day 8 dengan kebutuhan pencarian semantik." } },
      { title: "Embeddings and semantic search", source: "Hugging Face", topic: "Embeddings", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 45, url: "https://huggingface.co/learn", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Materi kursus tentang embedding kalimat dan pencarian semantik menggunakan model terbuka.",
          inti: ["Model embedding mengubah teks menjadi vektor.", "Kemiripan makna diukur secara numerik.", "Pemilihan model memengaruhi kualitas hasil.", "Pencarian semantik dan kata kunci saling melengkapi."],
          kenapa: "Memahami embedding membuat kualitas hasil RAG bisa didiagnosis, bukan hanya diterima.",
          hubungan: "Prasyarat langsung untuk Day 19 tentang RAG." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "Embeddings", q: "What does an embedding represent?", opts: ["A compressed copy of the original text", "A numeric vector positioning text by meaning, so similar meanings are close", "An encrypted version of the document", "The token count of a passage"], a: 1, e: "Embeddings make semantic similarity computable as distance. The original text is not recoverable from them in general." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Semantic vs keyword search", q: "Which query type is keyword search still better at?", opts: ["Conceptual questions", "Exact identifiers such as product or policy codes", "Paraphrased questions", "Multilingual queries"], a: 1, e: "Exact-match needs exact matching. Hybrid search exists because both approaches have distinct blind spots." },
      { t: "tf", c: "Chunking", q: "Chunk size has little effect on retrieval quality.", a: false, e: "It is one of the strongest levers: too large dilutes relevance, too small destroys meaning." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Chunking", q: "A retrieval system returns the correct entitlement table but for the wrong country. Most likely cause?", opts: ["The embedding model is broken", "The chunk was split from the heading identifying the jurisdiction, and no metadata preserved it", "The context window is too small", "The question was too short"], a: 1, e: "Meaning lost at chunk boundaries cannot be recovered downstream. Metadata and structure-aware splitting prevent it." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Semantic vs keyword search", q: "Why does semantic search find 'new starter induction' for a query about 'employee onboarding'?", opts: ["The words are spelled similarly", "Their embeddings are close because the meanings are close", "It translates the query first", "It uses a synonym dictionary"], a: 1, e: "Proximity in embedding space reflects learned semantic similarity, not lexical overlap." },
      { t: "sa", c: "Chunking", q: "Name two things a chunk should carry besides its text.", a: { groups: [["source", "sumber"], ["metadata", "metadata"], ["section", "heading", "bagian", "judul"], ["date", "tanggal"], ["url", "page", "halaman", "laman"]], need: 2, model: "Metadata such as source document, section or heading, date, and a link back to the original." }, e: "Provenance metadata is what makes citations and filtering possible." },
    ],
  },
  {
    day: 19,
    title: "RAG: Retrieval-Augmented Generation",
    sub: "rag",
    objectives: [
      "Describe the full RAG pipeline and where each failure can occur.",
      "Explain what RAG does and does not fix.",
      "Design a RAG evaluation that measures retrieval and generation separately.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "RAG answers a question by retrieving relevant passages from your own content and giving them to the model as context, instead of relying on what the model absorbed during training.",
        "The model's job becomes reading and synthesising supplied evidence, which is a task it is far more reliable at." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "The pipeline: documents are chunked, embedded and stored; a question is embedded; the nearest chunks are retrieved, optionally reranked; the question plus chunks go to the model with instructions to answer only from that context and to cite sources.",
        "Every stage can fail independently. Bad chunking, a weak embedding model, too few or too many retrieved chunks, a missing rerank, or an instruction that permits answering from general knowledge. When a RAG system gives a wrong answer, your first diagnostic question is whether the correct passage was retrieved at all — that splits the problem in half immediately.",
        "RAG does not eliminate hallucination; it constrains it and makes it auditable through citations. It also does not fix bad source content: if your policy documents contradict each other, the system will confidently reflect that contradiction.",
        "One distinction to fix now, because it is the most common misunderstanding: RAG changes nothing inside the model. The weights are untouched. All that changes is what appears in the context window at question time — retrieval writes text into the prompt. Fine-tuning is the other thing: it adjusts weights, costs orders of magnitude more, needs training data rather than documents, and cannot be updated the moment a policy changes. When your content changes weekly, RAG is the answer and fine-tuning is not." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Take ten realistic questions about a document set. For each, manually find the passage that should answer it. That is your retrieval ground truth." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Define two separate metrics: retrieval hit rate (was the right passage retrieved) and answer faithfulness (is the answer supported by the retrieved text). Explain why one number cannot express both." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "If a RAG assistant answers wrongly, what is the first thing you inspect, and why is it not the prompt?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "Splitting the diagnosis",
      body: "An internal assistant gives wrong answers about training entitlements. Logging the retrieved chunks shows the correct passage was never retrieved in seven of ten failures. No amount of prompt tuning would have fixed those cases — the fix was chunking and metadata filtering. In the remaining three, the passage was present but ignored, which is a generation problem. Two failures, two owners, one logging decision that made both visible.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Design a small RAG system on paper",
      steps: [
        "Choose a document set and define chunking, metadata and retrieval count.",
        "Write the system instruction, including citation and refusal behaviour.",
        "Define your evaluation set and both metrics before building anything.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Your organisation wants an AI assistant over 500 training documents, some outdated. Describe what you would fix before building anything, and what you would refuse to promise.",
    recap: {
      inti: "RAG menjawab pertanyaan dengan mengambil potongan relevan dari konten lo sendiri lalu menyerahkannya ke model sebagai konteks. Pipeline-nya: dokumen dipotong, di-embed, dan disimpan; pertanyaan di-embed; potongan terdekat diambil, kadang di-rerank; lalu pertanyaan beserta potongan itu dikirim ke model dengan instruksi menjawab hanya dari konteks tersebut dan menyebut sumbernya. Setiap tahap bisa gagal sendiri-sendiri, jadi pertanyaan diagnostik pertama selalu: apakah potongan yang benar berhasil diambil?",
      salahPaham: "RAG tidak mengubah apa pun di dalam model — weights-nya tidak tersentuh. Yang berubah hanya isi context window saat pertanyaan diajukan. Fine-tuning-lah yang mengubah weights, dan itu jauh lebih mahal, butuh data pelatihan alih-alih dokumen, serta tidak bisa diperbarui pada hari kebijakan lo berubah. RAG juga tidak memperbaiki sumber yang buruk: kalau dokumen lo saling bertentangan, sistemnya akan meneruskan pertentangan itu dengan percaya diri.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "retrieval", id: "pengambilan potongan paling relevan sebelum model menjawab" },
        { en: "rerank", id: "penyusunan ulang hasil pengambilan agar yang paling relevan naik" },
        { en: "fine-tuning", id: "penyetelan bobot model dengan data pelatihan; bukan yang dilakukan RAG" },
        { en: "citation", id: "penyebutan sumber yang membuat jawaban bisa diaudit" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "RAG concepts and implementation", source: "Hugging Face", topic: "RAG", difficulty: "Intermediate–Advanced", minutes: 60, url: "https://huggingface.co/learn", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Materi kursus tentang membangun sistem retrieval-augmented generation dengan komponen terbuka.",
          inti: ["Alur: chunking, embedding, penyimpanan, retrieval, generation.", "Peran reranking untuk meningkatkan relevansi.", "Instruksi agar model menjawab hanya dari konteks.", "Sitasi sumber sebagai mekanisme audit.", "Evaluasi retrieval terpisah dari evaluasi jawaban."],
          kenapa: "RAG adalah pola paling umum untuk membuat asisten AI berbasis pengetahuan organisasi.",
          hubungan: "Menggabungkan embeddings (Day 18), basis data (Day 8), API (Day 7), dan prompting (Day 17)." } },
      { title: "Retrieval and grounding guidance", source: "Google", topic: "RAG", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 30, url: "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi resmi tentang grounding jawaban model pada sumber data yang disediakan.",
          inti: ["Menyediakan konteks eksternal saat inference.", "Membatasi jawaban pada sumber yang diberikan.", "Menyertakan rujukan sumber.", "Batasan pendekatan grounding."],
          kenapa: "Menunjukkan implementasi resmi dari konsep RAG, bukan hanya teorinya.",
          hubungan: "Melengkapi materi hari ini dengan detail implementasi API." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "RAG pipeline", q: "What is the correct order of a basic RAG pipeline?", opts: ["Generate, retrieve, chunk, embed", "Chunk, embed, store, retrieve, generate", "Embed, generate, chunk, store", "Retrieve, chunk, generate, embed"], a: 1, e: "Indexing happens ahead of time; retrieval and generation happen per question." },
      { t: "tf", c: "RAG limitations", q: "RAG eliminates hallucination.", a: false, e: "It constrains generation to supplied evidence and enables citation, which reduces and exposes hallucination without removing it." },
      { t: "sc", c: "RAG diagnosis", q: "A RAG assistant answers incorrectly. What do you inspect first?", opts: ["The model's temperature", "Whether the correct passage was retrieved at all", "The user interface", "The size of the model"], a: 1, e: "That single check splits retrieval failures from generation failures, and the two have entirely different fixes." },
      { t: "mc", c: "RAG evaluation", q: "Why measure retrieval and generation separately?", opts: ["To produce more charts", "Because a single score hides which stage failed and who must fix it", "Because retrieval cannot be measured", "Because generation is always correct"], a: 1, e: "Separate metrics localise the fault, exactly as HTTP status codes localise integration faults." },
      { t: "mc", c: "RAG limitations", q: "Your source documents contradict each other. What does RAG do?", opts: ["Resolves the contradiction automatically", "Reflects the contradiction, often confidently", "Refuses to answer", "Rewrites the documents"], a: 1, e: "Content governance is a prerequisite, not something the system fixes for you." },
      { t: "sc", c: "RAG vs fine-tuning", q: "Your compliance policies are rewritten every few weeks and the assistant must always answer from the current version. A vendor proposes fine-tuning a model on the policy documents instead. What is the strongest objection?", opts: ["Fine-tuning cannot process text documents", "Fine-tuning adjusts weights, so every policy change means retraining — retrieval reads the current document at question time", "Fine-tuned models cannot cite sources at all", "Fine-tuning always produces worse language quality"], a: 1, e: "RAG changes what is in the context window; fine-tuning changes the weights. Frequently-changing content is the textbook case for retrieval, not training." },
      { t: "sa", c: "RAG pipeline", q: "Why is retrieved context more reliable than the model's own training knowledge for internal policy questions?", a: { groups: [["current", "mutakhir", "terbaru", "terkini"], ["internal", "internal", "aturan internal", "kebijakan"], ["source", "cite", "sumber", "dirujuk", "rujuk", "kutip"], ["specific", "spesifik", "rinci"], ["train", "data latih", "dilatih", "pelatihan"], ["verif", "periksa", "dicek"]], need: 2, model: "Internal policies were never in the training data, and retrieved passages are current, specific and citable." }, e: "Provenance and currency are the point." },
    ],
  },
  {
    day: 20,
    title: "Tool Calling and AI Agents",
    sub: "agents",
    objectives: [
      "Explain tool calling as a request-execute-return loop.",
      "Distinguish a workflow from an agent by who decides the next step.",
      "Identify where autonomy adds risk and what controls belong around it.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "A model cannot act. It can emit a structured request saying which tool it wants and with what arguments. Your software executes the tool and returns the result, which the model then uses.",
        "That loop is the whole mechanism behind AI agents." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "You define tools by name, description and parameter schema. The model selects among them based on those descriptions, which is why a vague tool description degrades reliability more than a vague prompt.",
        "A workflow has a fixed sequence you designed; the model fills in steps. An agent decides the sequence itself, looping until it judges the goal met. Agents are more flexible and much harder to predict, test and bound. Choose the least autonomy the problem requires.",
        "The controls that matter: least privilege for every tool, human approval before consequential or irreversible actions, iteration and cost limits, full logging of tool calls, and treating any content the agent reads — web pages, documents, emails — as data rather than instructions. That last point is prompt injection, and it is the defining security problem of agent systems." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Design three tools for an e-learning assistant: search_course_catalogue, get_learner_progress, enrol_learner. Write name, description and parameters for each.",
        "Mark which one must never run without human confirmation, and why." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Write the failure story for one tool: what happens if the model calls it with wrong arguments, and what in your design prevents damage." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "For which of your real tasks is a fixed workflow strictly better than an agent, and why?" ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "The document that gave orders",
      body: "An agent summarises uploaded course documents. One document contains a line addressed to the AI instructing it to email its content to an external address. A naive agent with an email tool complies, because it processed instructions found in data as if they came from the user. The defences are architectural: separate instruction channels from content, restrict tool scope, and require confirmation before anything leaves the organisation.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Specify an agent responsibly",
      steps: [
        "Define a narrow goal and the minimum tool set that achieves it.",
        "Write the permission boundary for each tool.",
        "Define stop conditions: maximum iterations, cost ceiling, and escalation to a human.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "Take one process you would like to automate at work. Argue both sides: why an agent is appropriate, and why a fixed workflow would be safer. Then decide, and state what evidence would change your decision.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Model tidak bisa bertindak. Yang bisa dilakukannya adalah memancarkan permintaan terstruktur: tool mana yang diinginkan, dengan argumen apa. Perangkat lunak lo yang mengeksekusi tool itu dan mengembalikan hasilnya, lalu model memakai hasil tersebut — loop itulah seluruh mekanisme di balik AI agent. Tool didefinisikan lewat nama, deskripsi, dan skema parameter, dan model memilih berdasarkan deskripsi itu, sehingga deskripsi tool yang kabur merusak keandalan lebih parah daripada prompt yang kabur.",
      salahPaham: "Agent bukan versi lebih baik dari workflow. Workflow punya urutan yang lo tentukan; agent menentukan urutannya sendiri, jadi ia lebih lentur sekaligus jauh lebih sulit diprediksi, diuji, dan dibatasi. Pilih otonomi paling sedikit yang memang dibutuhkan masalahnya. Dan perlakukan semua yang dibaca agent — halaman web, dokumen, email — sebagai data, bukan instruksi; itu inti dari prompt injection.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "tool calling", id: "permintaan terstruktur dari model untuk menjalankan sebuah fungsi" },
        { en: "agent", id: "sistem yang membiarkan model menentukan langkah berikutnya sendiri" },
