    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "Tool calling", q: "Who actually executes a tool the model requests?", opts: ["The model itself", "Your application code", "The user's browser automatically", "The tool provider's model"], a: 1, e: "The model emits a structured request. Execution — and therefore control and permission — stays with your software." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Workflow vs agent", q: "What distinguishes an agent from a workflow?", opts: ["Agents use larger models", "The agent decides the sequence of steps; a workflow's sequence is designed in advance", "Workflows cannot call tools", "Agents never need human oversight"], a: 1, e: "Autonomy over sequencing is the dividing line, and the source of both flexibility and unpredictability." },
      { t: "tf", c: "Agent safety", q: "Content an agent reads from a document can safely be treated as instructions.", a: false, e: "That is prompt injection. Retrieved content is data; instructions come from the user through a controlled channel." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Agent safety", q: "Which control best limits damage from an agent that enrols learners?", opts: ["A larger context window", "Human confirmation before the enrolment action executes", "A more detailed system prompt", "Running the agent more frequently"], a: 1, e: "Consequential and hard-to-reverse actions need a human checkpoint, not just better instructions." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Tool calling", q: "Why does a vague tool description hurt reliability?", opts: ["It increases token cost only", "The model selects tools based on their descriptions, so ambiguity causes wrong selections", "It breaks the JSON schema", "It slows execution"], a: 1, e: "Tool descriptions are the model's only basis for choosing. They are part of the interface, not documentation." },
      { t: "sa", c: "Workflow vs agent", q: "Give one reason to prefer a fixed workflow over an agent.", a: { groups: [["predict", "ramal", "diramalkan", "terduga", "pasti"], ["test", "uji", "diuji"], ["cost", "biaya"], ["control", "kendali", "dikendalikan", "terkontrol"], ["simple", "sederhana"], ["audit", "audit", "jejak"], ["safe", "bound", "aman", "terjaga"]], need: 2, model: "Fixed workflows are predictable, testable, cheaper and easier to audit — choose the least autonomy the problem requires." }, e: "Autonomy is a cost as well as a capability." },
    ],
  },
];

const CUR_F = [
  {
    day: 21,
    title: "Evaluating AI Systems",
    sub: "eval",
    objectives: [
      "Turn a vague quality goal into success criteria that can be measured.",
      "Choose between code-based, human and LLM-based grading for a given check.",
      "Separate a retrieval failure from a generation failure when a pipeline answers wrongly.",
    ],
    segments: [
      { m: 10, t: "Concept introduction", body: [
        "An evaluation — an eval — is a fixed set of test cases plus a defined way of grading them. It is the difference between 'this prompt feels better' and 'this prompt scores 84% against the same 60 cases the old one scored 71% on'.",
        "The reason evals exist is regression: a change that fixes the case in front of you routinely breaks three cases you are not looking at. Without a fixed set you never find out. Iterating on impressions feels fast and quietly is not." ] },
      { m: 20, t: "Core material", body: [
        "Start with success criteria, not with metrics. Good criteria are specific ('the summary names every policy clause referenced in the source'), measurable, achievable given current models, and relevant to what the application is actually for. 'Accurate' and 'high quality' are not criteria; they are placeholders for criteria you have not written yet.",
        "There are three ways to grade, and the order matters. Code-based grading — exact match, string presence, a schema check — is fastest, cheapest and most reliable, so use it wherever the task allows. Human grading is the highest quality and the least scalable; spend it where judgement is genuinely required. LLM-based grading, often called LLM-as-a-judge, sits between them: fast and flexible, but it is a model with the same failure modes as the model under test, so give it an explicit rubric, ask it to reason before scoring, and validate the judge itself against a set of human-labelled cases before trusting it at scale.",
        "For a pipeline, evaluate the components separately or you will misdiagnose everything. A RAG answer can be wrong because the right passage was never retrieved, or because the passage was retrieved and the model ignored it. Those are different bugs with different fixes, and one overall score hides which one you have. Frameworks such as Ragas exist precisely to split that measurement: context precision and context recall describe retrieval, faithfulness and answer relevancy describe generation.",
        "Two practical notes. Prefer volume over polish: fifty automatically graded cases beat eight hand-graded ones for catching regressions. And build the eval set from real failures — the questions your users actually asked and got a bad answer to — rather than from cases you invented, which tend to be the cases you already handle." ] },
      { m: 15, t: "Hands-on", body: [
        "Take the short-answer grader in this very app. Write five answers you consider correct and five you consider wrong for the same question, then run them mentally against the keyword groups shown in the answer review after a post-test.",
        "Count how many of the ten it gets right. You have just built the smallest possible eval, and the number you get is more informative than any impression you had about the grader before." ] },
      { m: 10, t: "Practice", body: [
        "Write three success criteria for an AI that generates quiz questions from a lesson. Each must contain a number or a rule a second person could apply without asking you what you meant.",
        "Then mark each one with the grading method you would use: code, human, or model." ] },
      { m: 5, t: "Reflection", body: [
        "Which of your three criteria turned out to need a human? If all three did, look again — one of them can probably be reduced to a rule." ] },
    ],
    example: {
      t: "A judge that needed judging",
      body: "A team building a summariser for policy documents used an LLM judge scoring 1-5 on 'faithfulness', and their scores looked healthy. When someone finally hand-labelled forty of the judged cases, the judge agreed with the humans on roughly three out of four, and its disagreements were not random — it rewarded fluent summaries that had dropped a qualifying clause. The fix was not a better model but a sharper rubric: score 1 automatically if any clause in the source is absent from the summary. The transferable lesson is that an unvalidated judge does not remove subjectivity, it hides it behind a number.",
    },
    practice: {
      t: "Build a ten-case eval",
      steps: [
        "Pick one AI task you would plausibly build: quiz generation, summarising, or answering questions over your own documents.",
        "Write ten inputs with the output you would accept, including at least three edge cases and one input that should be refused or flagged.",
        "For each case, name the grading method and write the rule the grader applies. Any case where you cannot write the rule is a case where your criteria are still vague.",
      ],
    },
    challenge: "This app's own documentation claims its short-answer grader wrongly accepts off-topic answers about 1.8% of the time. Before reading how that was produced, design the eval that would generate such a number: what dataset, what counts as off-topic, who or what grades it, and what would make the figure misleading. Then compare your design to the one described in the project notes.",
    recap: {
      inti: "Eval adalah sekumpulan kasus uji tetap ditambah cara penilaian yang jelas. Gunanya bukan membuktikan sistem lo bagus, melainkan menangkap regresi: perubahan yang memperbaiki satu kasus sering merusak tiga kasus lain yang tidak sedang lo lihat. Mulai dari success criteria yang spesifik dan bisa diukur, baru pilih cara menilainya — kode paling murah dan paling andal, manusia paling berkualitas dan paling mahal, model paling lentur tapi harus divalidasi dulu terhadap label manusia. Untuk pipeline, nilai tiap komponen terpisah.",
      salahPaham: "LLM-as-a-judge bukan wasit netral. Ia model dengan kelemahan yang sama dengan model yang sedang diuji, jadi skornya bukan objektivitas — ia subjektivitas yang disembunyikan di balik angka sampai lo membandingkannya dengan penilaian manusia.",
      istilah: [
        { en: "eval", id: "kumpulan kasus uji tetap beserta cara penilaiannya" },
        { en: "success criteria", id: "kriteria keberhasilan yang spesifik dan bisa diukur" },
        { en: "LLM-as-a-judge", id: "memakai model lain sebagai penilai; perlu rubrik dan validasi" },
        { en: "regression", id: "perbaikan di satu tempat yang diam-diam merusak tempat lain" },
        { en: "faithfulness", id: "apakah jawaban benar-benar didukung konteks yang diberikan" },
      ],
    },
    resources: [
      { title: "Define success criteria and build evaluations", source: "Anthropic (Claude Platform Docs)", topic: "AI Evaluation", difficulty: "Beginner–Intermediate", minutes: 30, url: "https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Dokumentasi resmi tentang cara menetapkan kriteria keberhasilan dan menyusun eval untuk aplikasi berbasis LLM, lengkap dengan contoh tiap metode penilaian.",
          inti: ["Kriteria yang baik bersifat spesifik, terukur, realistis, dan relevan.", "Tiga cara menilai: berbasis kode, manusia, dan model (LLM-as-a-judge).", "Utamakan jumlah kasus daripada kesempurnaan tiap kasus.", "Untuk penilaian oleh model: pakai rubrik jelas, minta penalaran dulu baru skor.", "Rancang eval yang meniru distribusi tugas nyata, termasuk kasus pinggiran."],
          kenapa: "Ini satu-satunya materi hari ini yang langsung bisa dipakai besok: formatnya praktis, bukan teori evaluasi.",
          hubungan: "Melanjutkan Day 13 (metrik dan overfitting) dan Day 19 (evaluasi RAG dipisah antara retrieval dan generation). URL sudah dibuka dan diverifikasi pada sesi ini." } },
      { title: "List of available metrics", source: "Ragas (dokumentasi)", topic: "AI Evaluation", difficulty: "Intermediate", minutes: 25, url: "https://docs.ragas.io/en/stable/concepts/metrics/available_metrics/", verified: true,
        id: { apa: "Katalog metrik evaluasi untuk sistem RAG dan agent: apa yang diukur tiap metrik dan komponen mana yang disorotnya.",
          inti: ["Context precision dan context recall menilai tahap retrieval.", "Faithfulness dan response relevancy menilai tahap generation.", "Ada metrik khusus untuk agent, misalnya ketepatan pemanggilan tool.", "Sebagian metrik dihitung memakai LLM, jadi ikut menanggung kelemahan LLM.", "Metrik tradisional seperti BLEU dan ROUGE tetap tersedia untuk pembandingan teks."],
          kenapa: "Memberi kosakata konkret untuk memisahkan kegagalan retrieval dari kegagalan generation — pertanyaan diagnostik pertama di Day 19.",
          hubungan: "Ragas adalah pustaka open-source pihak ketiga, bukan standar resmi; pakai definisi metriknya sebagai kerangka berpikir, bukan sebagai satu-satunya cara. URL sudah dibuka dan diverifikasi pada sesi ini." } },
    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "Eval design", q: "What is the main thing a fixed set of test cases buys you that trying a prompt and reading the output does not?", opts: ["Faster responses from the model", "The ability to detect that a change fixed one case and broke others", "Lower API costs", "A guarantee the model is correct"], a: 1, e: "Regression detection is the core value. Impressions cover the case in front of you; a fixed set covers the cases you are not looking at." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Success criteria", q: "Which of these is usable as a success criterion?", opts: ["The summary should be high quality", "The summary should read naturally", "Every policy clause referenced in the source appears in the summary", "The model should understand the document"], a: 2, e: "A criterion has to be checkable by someone who cannot ask you what you meant. Only the third one is." },
      { t: "tf", c: "Grading methods", q: "An LLM used as a judge produces objective scores, so it does not need to be validated itself.", a: false, e: "The judge is a model with the same failure modes as the model under test. Until you compare it against human-labelled cases, its scores are hidden subjectivity, not objectivity." },
      { t: "sc", c: "Component evaluation", q: "Your RAG assistant answers a policy question incorrectly. What is the first thing to check?", opts: ["Whether the model needs fine-tuning", "Whether the correct passage was retrieved at all", "Whether the temperature setting is too high", "Whether the context window is too small"], a: 1, e: "That single check splits the problem in half: retrieval failure and generation failure are different bugs with different fixes." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Grading methods", q: "You need to check that a model's output is valid JSON matching a schema. Which grading method fits?", opts: ["Human grading, because structure requires judgement", "LLM-based grading with a rubric", "Code-based grading", "No grading is needed if the prompt asks for JSON"], a: 2, e: "Where a rule can be written, code grades it faster, cheaper and more reliably than either humans or models. Save the expensive methods for genuine judgement." },
      { t: "sa", c: "Eval design", q: "In your own words: why is a fixed set of test cases better than trying a prompt and judging the result by eye?", a: { groups: [["consistent", "konsisten", "sama"], ["compare", "banding"], ["regress", "break", "rusak"], ["subjective", "subjektif", "kesan", "perasaan"]], need: 2, model: "A fixed set lets you compare versions consistently and catch the cases a change breaks, instead of judging from a few subjective impressions." }, e: "The two ideas that matter are consistent comparison across versions and catching regressions you were not looking for." },
    ],
