    ],
    quiz: [
      { t: "mc", c: "MCP problem", q: "What problem does MCP primarily solve?", opts: ["Models being too slow at tool calling", "Every AI application needing its own custom connector for every system", "Models hallucinating tool results", "The cost of running models locally"], a: 1, e: "It is an integration problem, not a model problem. Ten applications and ten systems previously meant a hundred pieces of bespoke glue." },
      { t: "mc", c: "Server capabilities", q: "Which three things can an MCP server expose?", opts: ["Models, embeddings and tokens", "Resources, prompts and tools", "Databases, files and APIs", "Clients, hosts and transports"], a: 1, e: "Resources are context and data to read, prompts are templated workflows for the user, and tools are functions the model can execute." },
      { t: "tf", c: "MCP security", q: "Because MCP is an open standard with a specification, using MCP servers is inherently safer than writing your own tool calls.", a: false, e: "The specification says the opposite: tools are arbitrary code execution, tool descriptions should be treated as untrusted unless the server is, and hosts must get explicit consent. A standard defines the interface, not the trustworthiness of who implements it." },
      { t: "sc", c: "MCP security", q: "A setup guide for a community MCP server asks for an API token, and the simplest option is a full-scope token. What should you do?", opts: ["Use the full-scope token, since the server is open source", "Create the narrowest token the server actually needs, usually read-only", "Skip the token and run the server without authentication", "Use your personal account token so it is easier to revoke"], a: 1, e: "Least privilege from Day 22, with a supply-chain dimension: you are running someone else's code with whatever authority you hand it." },
      { t: "mc", c: "MCP roles", q: "In MCP terminology, what is a host?", opts: ["The server that provides data", "The LLM application that initiates connections", "The cloud provider running the workload", "The database behind the integration"], a: 1, e: "Hosts initiate; clients are the connectors inside them; servers provide context and capabilities." },
      { t: "sa", c: "MCP problem", q: "In your own words: what does MCP standardise, and why does that matter?", a: { groups: [["connect", "koneksi", "sambung", "integrasi"], ["reuse", "ulang", "sekali tulis"], ["standar", "protokol", "protocol"], ["setiap", "every", "masing-masing"]], need: 2, model: "MCP standardises how AI applications connect to external systems, so one server can be reused by any compliant client instead of every application writing its own connector for every system." }, e: "The two ideas are a standard connection interface and reuse across applications, replacing per-application integration work." },
